Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to build a
Python command-line tool with the following specification: Create a Python 3
command-line script for Termux (Android) that crawls a web page and extracts the
hyperlinks it contains.
The script should be runnable with a shebang line pointing to the Termux Python
interpreter (`/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`) and use `from
__future__ import annotations` for type hints.
It must depend on the `requests` and `beautifulsoup4` (bs4) libraries, using the
`html.parser` backend.
Functional requirements: - Accept a URL as a required positional command-line
argument, parsed with `argparse`.
Provide additional options as needed to control the output file path and any
other behavior the implementation requires. - Build a `requests.Session` through
a factory function that sets a realistic desktop browser User-Agent and matching
headers: Accept (HTML/XHTML/XML with image/webp and a fallback wildcard),
Accept-Language favoring English with Persian as a secondary option,
Accept-Encoding of gzip, deflate, and br, Connection keep-alive,
Upgrade-Insecure-Requests set to 1, and Cache-Control max-age=0. - Fetch the
target URL with a 30-second timeout, following redirects, and call
`raise_for_status()` so HTTP errors surface as exceptions. - Set the response
encoding to the detected apparent encoding, falling back to UTF-8 when detection
returns nothing. - Parse the HTML with BeautifulSoup and collect every `<a>` tag
that has an `href` attribute. - Strip whitespace from each href.
Skip empty hrefs, fragment-only links starting with `#`, and `javascript:`
pseudo-links. - Resolve each remaining href to an absolute URL relative to the
page URL using `urljoin`. - Parse the resolved URLs with `urlparse` and keep
only those with an http or https scheme and a non-empty network location, so
mailto, tel, data, and similar schemes are excluded. - Deduplicate results using
a set, then output the links in sorted order. - Write the sorted links one per
line to the output destination, and print a summary or the links to standard
output as appropriate for the design. - Optionally handle the output path as a
`pathlib.Path`, creating parent directories if they do not exist.
Error handling and edge cases: - Handle network failures, timeouts, and HTTP
error statuses gracefully, reporting a clear message to standard error and
exiting with a non-zero status code rather than printing a raw traceback. -
Handle a page with no qualifying links by reporting that no links were found,
without crashing. - Handle malformed or relative URLs, unusual schemes, and
whitespace-padded hrefs as described above. - Handle file write errors
(permissions, invalid path) with a clear message and non-zero exit. - Handle
keyboard interrupt cleanly.
Execution: the script should be executable directly from the command line and
exit with status 0 on success and a non-zero status on any failure.
Include a `main()` function guarded by `if __name__ == "__main__":` and return
an integer exit code from it passed to `sys.exit`.
Use only the standard library plus `requests` and `beautifulsoup4`. ---
